	<h2>The Editorial Axiom</h2>
	<p>
		<strong>AI can answer questions. ErrorLedger tells you what happened.</strong>
	</p>
	<p>
		The modern web is flooded with generic information retrieval. When something catastrophic happens—a $440M trading algorithm suicide, an intern scapegoated for a production wipeout, or a global cloud blackout—press releases offer diplomatic sanitization and AI summaries offer bullet points.
	</p>
	<p>
		<strong>ErrorLedger is different.</strong> We are an investigative, narrative-driven archive of real human, corporate, and systemic mistakes. We investigate how reasonable people, flawed incentives, exhaustion, and hidden systemic pressures combine to produce catastrophic or bizarre outcomes.
	</p>

	<h2>The Brand Promise</h2>
	<blockquote>
		<strong>Every mistake leaves a story.</strong><br/>
		<em>Real failures. Strange decisions. Unbelievable consequences.</em>
	</blockquote>

	<h2>The Archivist: Keeping the Receipts</h2>
	<p>
		ErrorLedger is curated under the editorial direction of <strong>The Archivist</strong>. 
	</p>
	<p>
		The Archivist operates with a clear worldview: <em>people rarely set out to cause disasters.</em> Most catastrophes are born from fear, ego, misaligned corporate incentives, or late-night exhaustion. 
	</p>
	<p>
		Every investigation concludes with <strong>The Archivist’s Verdict</strong>—a sharp, objective dissection of the true root cause that looks past superficial blame to expose the underlying organizational pathology.
	</p>

	<h2>Our Core Storytelling Archetypes</h2>
	<p>Every case in the Ledger is crafted around five structural narrative archetypes:</p>
	<ul>
		<li><strong>The Confession:</strong> First-person vulnerability, secret anxiety, and the snowballing escalation of a hidden mistake.</li>
		<li><strong>The Incident:</strong> High-rigor, journalistic reconstruction of a documented corporate, legal, or technology disaster.</li>
		<li><strong>The Verdict:</strong> High-stakes moral dilemmas exploring ambiguous blame, competing loyalties, and corporate scapegoats.</li>
		<li><strong>The Investigation:</strong> The forensic discovery arc peeling back deceptive layers to uncover the hidden catalyst.</li>
		<li><strong>The Failure Anatomy:</strong> Deconstructing how catastrophic outcomes became mathematically inevitable through chains of minor compromises.</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>The Reader Verdict Protocol</h2>
	<p>
		Mistakes are rarely black and white. ErrorLedger integrates the <strong>Reader Verdict</strong> interactive poll on every incident, inviting the community to evaluate moral culpability and record collective consensus on who bears true responsibility.
	</p>

	<h2>Whistleblower & Intake Protocols</h2>
	<p>
		We welcome anonymous submissions from engineers, operators, and professionals who have lived through operational or workplace catastrophes. All submissions undergo strict scrub cycles to remove identifying credentials prior to editorial review.
	</p>
